  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nSome residual light rain showers were moving slowly eastward across \neastern Kansas this afternoon while continuing to slowly diminish as \nthe LLJ/mid-level waa weakens. A capping inversion is anticipated in \nthe wake of the shower activity with little or no additional \ndevelopment anticipated in it's wake.\n\nAs we move into the weekend, the mid/upper ridge over the Rockies \nand Intermountain Region is progged to translate eastward edging \ninto the Central Plains. This is expected to result in mostly dry \nweather conditions while bringing an increasing threat for excessive \nheat. Afternoon heat indices are expected to climb into the 105-110 \nrange for much of the area and a heat advisory is in effect for all \nof the region from Saturday through Tuesday. There may be an upgrade \nto an excessive heat warning in parts of southeast Kansas where \nhumidity may remain higher. \n\nA shortwave trough may serve to break down the ridge as we move into \nthe Tue-Wed timeframe as a frontal boundary tries to sink southward \ninto Kansas before becoming diffuse or stationary bringing some low \npops into parts of central and northern Kansas.\n\nThe mid/upper ridge is also progged to retrograde into the Rockies \nlater in the week bringing some subtle relief from the heat (above \nnormal temperatures continuing) with some isolated or widely \nscattered nocturnal storm activity associated with the LLJ.",
